1. In the context of multi-dimensional feature spaces, which statement accurately describes 
   the geometric representation of a Linear Regression model? 
   A. It is always represented by a straight line regardless of dimensionality. 
   B. It takes the form of a straight line only when the variable x is one-dimensional 
      and becomes a hyperplane when multi-dimensional. 
   C. It forms a non-linear curved plane to minimize the empirical error. 
   D. It uses a circular boundary to enclose all relevant data points.

2. Regarding Polynomial Regression, why is it technically classified as a type of 
   "Linear Regression" even when it handles non-linear datasets? 
   A. Because it uses the Sigmoid function to linearize outputs. 
   B. Because it only applies to datasets that can be split by a single vertical line. 
   C. Although its features are non-linear (e.g., x2,xn), the mathematical relationship 
      between its weight parameters w remains linear. 
   D. It is actually not classified as linear regression; it is a type of unsupervised clustering.

3. True or False: Linear regression assumes that the prediction error ε follows a normal 
   (Gaussian) distribution, and its loss function can be derived using maximum 
   likelihood estimation (MLE).

4. When comparing L1 (Lasso) and L2 (Ridge) regularization in Linear Regression, 
   which of the following is true? (Select all that apply) 
   A. L2 regularization adds a square sum loss to the target function. 
   B. L1 regularization can reduce parameter values directly to 0, effectively performing 
      feature selection. 
   C. L2 regularization reduces parameters to values close to 0 but does not typically 
      reduce them to exactly 0. 
   D. Lasso regression is exclusively used for classification tasks.

5. Despite its name, what is the primary function of the Logistic Regression model? 
   A. It is a continuous regression model used for predicting exact house prices. 
   B. It is a classification model used to resolve binary or multiclass classification problems. 
   C. It is a self-supervised model that generates new labels for unlabeled data. 
   D. It is an unsupervised clustering algorithm based on K-means logic.

6. How does Logistic Regression introduce non-linearity into a "broad sense" linear model? 
   A. By adding multiple hidden layers like a deep neural network. 
   B. By using the kernel trick to map data to a high-dimensional space. 
   C. By introducing a Sigmoid function that maps inputs to an output 
      interval of (0, 1) and setting a threshold. 
   D. By calculating the mean absolute error instead of the mean squared error.

7. True or False: The Softmax function is a generalization of Logistic Regression 
   used for k-class classification, ensuring the sum of all predicted probabilities
   for the classes equals 1.

8. Which of the following is the standard loss function used for training Logistic 
   Regression models in deep learning frameworks? 
   A. Mean Squared Error (MSE). 
   B. Cross-Entropy Loss. 
   C. Hinge Loss. 
   D. L1 Absolute Error.

9. What is the core mathematical objective of a Linear SVM? 
   A. To find a line that passes through the average of all data points. 
   B. To minimize the distance between the centroids of two clusters. 
   C. To find an optimal hyperplane that maximizes the width of the 
      gap (margin) between two categories in the feature space. 
   D. To map all data points to a zero-dimensional point.

10. How does the "Kernel Trick" allow an SVM to handle linearly inseparable datasets? 
   A. It removes the outliers from the training set to make the data linear. 
   B. It uses non-linear mapping algorithms to convert low-dimensional inseparable
      samples into a high-dimensional feature space where they become separable. 
   C. It replaces the weights with support vectors to reduce computational cost. 
   D. It converts the classification task into a regression task using the Sigmoid function.

11. Which points in a dataset are specifically referred to as "Support Vectors"?
   A. All data points located in the center of a cluster. 
   B. The points that are furthest away from the decision boundary. 
   C. The most nearby points to the decision line/hyperplane which determine its position. 
   D. Points that have been misclassified by the model during the training phase.

